  <%# == Connection state, as an indicator rather than an essay (SPGD-705)
      ==========================================================================================

      This is what survives of the "Connect this repository" panel's Connection stat: the same
      states, the same order, the same reasoning about which one wins — and one clause each instead
      of a `UI::StatComponent` plus three paragraphs of prose about HTTP status codes.

      What was dropped was dropped because it was explaining, in words, a table that sits further
      down this same page. "Rejected deliveries" lists every refused delivery with its reason; a
      panel above it teaching the reader to distinguish a 401 from a 400 by reasoning about which
      one stamps `last_used_at` was a manual for a surface the reader can simply look at. The
      refusing state links straight to it instead.

      == The five states, and why they are in this order

      Each of the first four is a strictly more specific condition than the one after it, so the
      order is not a preference — reversing any adjacent pair would report the vaguer answer for a
      repository the page can say something exact about.

      1. REVOKED KEY STILL PRESENTED. A key this page's owner revoked has arrived at the API and
         been refused, which stamped `last_refused_at` on the row `revoke!` kept (SPGD-804: a
         revoked key is retired, not deleted, precisely so this state is observable). It outranks
         everything below it, REFUSING included, and the reason is temporal, not a tone preference:
         a pipeline presenting a revoked token cannot complete a delivery at all, so any refusals
         on record predate the problem this state names and describe a pipeline that was at least
         getting in. Reporting the vaguer state would send the owner to debug payloads while every
         request is dying at the door. `:error` on state 2's own rule — work is being destroyed,
         not merely absent — and the fix is named because it is off this page: the token lives in
         someone else's secret store.
         This state is bounded by what the platform can see, and the bound is stated on the
         surface: `last_refused_at` is the LAST observed presentation, so the note dates it rather
         than claiming the client is presenting right now; and a key revoked and never presented
         again produces no row here — nothing is synthesized for it.

      2. REFUSING. `@last_api_request_at` is `api_keys.last_used_at`, stamped by
         `Api::BaseController#authenticate_api_key!` on the way IN, before the payload is looked at.
         So it answers "did anything authenticate" and nothing else, and read alone it produced this
         product's own vacuous green: a repository whose every run was refused for its payload read
         "Connected", in success tone, over a pipeline throwing away 100% of its work. A delivery
         being refused HAS authenticated, so this state implies the next one and must precede it.
         `:error` rather than `:warning` because work is being destroyed, not merely absent.

      3. CONNECTED. `@last_live_api_request_at` is the same figure restricted to keys still carrying
         the token that stamped them. Restricted, because `regenerate!` retires a token with no
         grace window and deliberately leaves `last_used_at` standing (it is the key's history), so
         the unrestricted figure reports an age belonging to a credential that no longer exists.

      4. ROTATED, NOT YET IN USE. Reached only when something authenticated but nothing live did —
         i.e. every key that ever authenticated has since been rotated. Dated from the OLDEST
         rotation in the set, which is the only date true of all of them at once: the NEWEST would
         make "nothing has authenticated since <date>" read most reassuringly on the one indicator
         that exists to stop reading reassuringly, and would date a five-day-dead pipeline at one
         minute whenever a second key was rotated just now.

      5. NOT CONNECTED YET. No LIVE key has ever authenticated. `:neutral`, not `:warning`: a
         repository registered a minute ago has nothing wrong with it, and this is the only one of
         the five that is an ordinary starting condition rather than a fault. Its note says "no
         ACTIVE API key has been used" rather than "no API key has been used" — the note is a claim,
         and since SPGD-804 revocations are retained rows whose history can contradict the
         unqualified sentence. The revoked-key state above has already named any presentation the
         platform saw; this branch is what the owner reads when CI has gone quiet on top of a
         revocation.

      Ungated, like its predecessor and like the Overview and Rejected-deliveries panels: a member
      with `view` alone legitimately needs to know whether CI is reaching this repository, and
      nothing here is credential metadata — no key name, no hint, no token. %>
  <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2" id="connection-indicator">
    <% if @presented_revoked_api_keys.any? %>
      <%# `revocations` cannot be empty here: the set is restricted to rows carrying a refusal
          stamp, and a revoked row is stamped `revoked_at` at the moment of its revocation — every
          row in the set has both. `revocations.first` is the OLDEST revocation when there are
          several, on the rule state 4 states for rotations: the only date true of all of them at
          once. `last_refused_at` is the NEWEST stamp, because there the question the reader is
          asking is "is this happening now", and the freshest observed presentation is the honest
          answer. %>
      <% revocations = @presented_revoked_api_keys.filter_map(&:revoked_at).sort %>
      <% last_refusal = @presented_revoked_api_keys.filter_map(&:last_refused_at).max %>
      <%= render UI::BadgeComponent.new(tone: :error) { "Revoked key still presented" } %>
      <span class="text-xs text-app-muted">
        <% if revocations.one? %>
          A key you revoked <%= time_ago_in_words(revocations.first) %> ago is still being presented
          — last seen <%= time_ago_in_words(last_refusal) %> ago. Update the secret wherever it is
          stored.
        <% else %>
          <%= revocations.size %> keys you revoked are still being presented — the first was revoked
          <%= time_ago_in_words(revocations.first) %> ago, the latest attempt was seen
          <%= time_ago_in_words(last_refusal) %> ago. Update the secrets wherever they are stored.
        <% end %>
      </span>
    <% elsif @rejected_ingests.refusing? %>
      <%# THE SAME SEAMS THE REPOSITORIES GRID RENDERS — `refused_deliveries_label` and
          `refused_deliveries_note` own the words, so a card and the page it links to cannot word
          one repository's refusal two different ways, and the next rename of either cannot move one
          surface without the other. That is the rule `test_run_delivery_note` and
          `test_run_cost_rows` are already held to on this page's sibling.

          The seams own the WORDING and not the markup: this surface and the card put the same
          sentence in different containers, so each wraps it itself. The link below stays here
          rather than going into the seam — it points at a panel that exists on THIS page only, and
          the grid links to the page rather than to an anchor inside it. %>
      <%= render UI::BadgeComponent.new(tone: :error) { refused_deliveries_label } %>
      <span class="text-xs text-app-muted">
        <%= refused_deliveries_note(@rejected_ingests.last_rejection_at) %> See
        <a href="#rejected-ingests" class="underline">Rejected deliveries</a>.
      </span>
    <% elsif @last_live_api_request_at.present? %>
      <%= render UI::BadgeComponent.new(tone: :success) { "Connected" } %>
      <span class="text-xs text-app-muted">
        Last request <%= time_ago_in_words(@last_live_api_request_at) %> ago.
      </span>
    <% elsif @last_api_request_at.present? %>
      <%# `rotations` cannot be empty here: something authenticated and nothing live did, so the key
          holding that use is stranded, and a stranded key has a `rotated_at`. %>
      <% rotations = @rotated_unused_api_keys.filter_map(&:rotated_at).sort %>
      <%= render UI::BadgeComponent.new(tone: :warning) { "Key rotated, not yet in use" } %>
      <span class="text-xs text-app-muted">
        <% if rotations.one? %>
          Nothing has authenticated since the key was regenerated
          <%= time_ago_in_words(rotations.first) %> ago — the replacement token has not reached CI.
          Update it wherever the old one was stored.
        <% else %>
          <%= rotations.size %> keys have been regenerated with nothing authenticating since; the
          oldest was regenerated <%= time_ago_in_words(rotations.first) %> ago — the replacement
          tokens have not reached CI. Update them wherever the old ones were stored.
        <% end %>
      </span>
    <% else %>
      <%= render UI::BadgeComponent.new(tone: :neutral) { "Not connected yet" } %>
      <%# ONE sentence for every sub-case that lands here — fresh repository, unused live keys,
          every key revoked — because the sentence is a claim and it has to survive all three.
          "No API key has been used" (the wording this branch carried before revocations were
          retained) would be false in the third: a revoked row's history says it WAS used. "No
          active API key has been used" is true of all three, and the revoked-key state above has
          already named any presentation the platform saw — so this branch is what the owner reads
          when CI has gone quiet on top of a revocation, and it must not dress that up as a
          repository that was never wired up. %>
      <span class="text-xs text-app-muted">No active API key has been used.</span>
    <% end %>
  </div>
